forget the election the battle for the  supreme court is one of the most  important in america  it won't just affect people's lives for  a few years  but for generations on the 18th of  september 2020  ruth bader ginsburg died and her spot on  the supreme court of the united states  became vacant this was a big shock to  politics in america  as a replacement needed to be appointed  and the person who got to nominate a  replacement  was president donald trump before she  died  ruth bader ginsburg asked for her  position to be filled  after the election but sticking to that  wish  would mean donald trump missing out on  one of the most influential  and lasting decisions a president can  make  as the highest court in the land it has  the final say on any laws which are  brought into question  it interprets the constitution which are  the core principles on which  america is based and more crucially  its nine justices serve for life so once  you're on the supreme court  you'll stay on it until you die holding  a seat  is one of the most powerful positions in  the country  political leanings can massively impact  the decision  someone makes as it stands the court is  split with  five conservative judges and three  liberal with one spot left to fill  and the appointment of a new member is  an opportunity to cement those beliefs  into laws for years to come  so who was ruth bader ginsburg and what  did she stand for  it was exhilarating to help bring down  the barriers  that in justice brennan's words put  women  less on a pedestal than in a cage  she was a liberal icon a heroine to the  american left  after overcoming entrenched sexism  she was a fierce advocate for women's  rights  and delivered landmark laws on abortion  same-sex  marriage immigration and health care  she fought against loosening of gun laws  viewing the second amendment as a relic  of the past  so how about president trump's nominee  today it is my honor to nominate  one of our nation's most brilliant and  gifted legal minds  to the supreme court amy coney barrett  is a devout catholic  and a conservative she's a judge and has  been a professor of law at notre dame  law school  she would also be the youngest supreme  court justice  and based on average life expectancy of  u.s women  would probably serve around 30 years  in short it's a massive chance for  donald trump to leave a mark  well past his term as president  and you think we should take the  president at his word  when he says his nominee will do the  right thing and overturn the affordable  care act  i can't really speak to what the  president has said on twitter  i'm not here on a mission to destroy the  affordable care act  but amy coney barrett's past judgments  show an almost uniformly conservative  voting record  in cases touching on abortion gun rights  discrimination and immigration  and there are many who believe her  appointment could be a threat  to democratic values president trump is  attempting to roll back  americans rights for decades to come  but a common defense is not to assume  how new judges will vote on the supreme  court  as members don't always vote the way  their history suggests  now for our part i i would never presume  how judge amy coney barrett  would rule on the supreme court for  example republican president dwight  eisenhower appointed earl warren to the  supreme court in 1953.  warren went on to preside over one of  the most liberal courts in u.s  history championing the racial  desegregation of schools  and upholding the civil rights act which  outlawed discrimination on the grounds  of race  color sex and religion eisenhower is  said to have called it  the biggest damn full mistake he ever  made  so how does the process work well once a  candidate is nominated they're  interviewed by the judiciary committee  this involves the nominee being asked  many  many  questions we've been grappling as a  nation with a lot of these  issues would you like to respond to that  at all before i go ahead  i'd like you just to clarify that point  sort of like a job interview  then the senate votes either appointing  or rejecting  the nominee this is an important choice  because it's an opportunity to  turn the political dial to the left or  the right and the appointment of amy  coney barrett would  stack the court 6'3 to the right  and it wouldn't be possible to redress  the balance until another spot became  free
 

